course: dictionary
lesson: 10198
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary gives each meaning its own number, so you can choose the one that fits the sentence. The word tine can mean a prong on a fork, or a branch on a deer's antler.
explain: Read the words around it to decide which meaning is right. «Each tip of an antler is called a point or tine» is about a deer, so tine here means a branch of the antler, not a fork. Always check: does my meaning fit this sentence?
explain: The same word can appear in different subjects. At home, a tine is the spike of a fork or rake. In nature lessons, it is a point on a stag's antler. In D and T, it is a sharp point on a tool. The subject you are in gives you a clue.
explain: Phrases help too. "The tines of a fork" and "antler tines" both tell you what the tine belongs to. Look at what the word is attached to, and the meaning becomes clear.
fact: A tine is one of the pointed prongs of a fork, rake or garden fork.
fact: A tine is also one of the pointed branches of a deer's antler.
fact: «Each tip of an antler is called a point or tine.»
fact: A big red deer stag can have twelve or more tines, which are also called points.
fact: Hunters called a twelve-pointer a royal, but the number of points does not show a stag's exact age.
exercise: «Each tip of an antler is called a point or tine.» What is a tine in this sentence? | *A pointed branch of a deer's antler | A prong of a garden fork | A sharp tool for digging | A kind of fork for eating
exercise: Which of these has tines in a nature lesson? | A garden hose | *A stag's antler | A bicycle wheel | A glass window
exercise: Look at the phrase "the tines of a fork". What does tine mean here? | The handle at the end of the fork | The metal bowl that holds the food | *One of the pointed prongs of the fork | A cup for drinking
test: «A big red deer stag can have twelve or more.» Twelve or more of what? | Years old | *Tines or points on its antlers | Hunters | Royal forks
test: Hunters called a twelve-pointer a royal. What does royal mean here? | A king's deer | A kind of fork | *A stag with twelve points on its antlers | A garden tool
exam: Tine has two meanings. Which two things can it be? | A rake and a spade | A stag and a hunter | A fork and a spoon | *A fork prong and a deer's antler point
